	    <div>
		<h3>Abstract</h3>
		This document reconstructs the Seasoning belief system as a field paper. Its core claim is simple: rave is not an imported spectacle, nor a lifestyle accessory, but a living folk practice carried by systems, memory, place and repeated gathering. What follows traces that claim through Stroud’s local ecology, through sister parties and partner crews across the UK, through the soundsystem understood as a boundary object, through hospitality to the stranger, and through the low-end, diasporic, technically adventurous listening culture that Seasoning has tried to hold open.
	    </div>
	    <div>
		<h2>I. Proposition</h2>
		<br>
		Seasoning began in Stroud with a simple conviction: that rave is not an imported spectacle or a lifestyle accessory, but a living practice held together by people, places, memory and repetition. To say <strong>rave culture is folk culture</strong> is to insist that this music belongs to the people who keep carrying it. Folk culture is not a museum object. It is a social technology. It survives by being handed on, bent, reworked, argued over and brought back to life in new rooms. Rave works the same way. A tune becomes a tool, a rig becomes a gathering point, a smoking area becomes a seminar, an old lockup full of records and half-broken gear becomes an archive.<span class="ref">1</span>
		<br><br>In Stroud, that archive is not only formal. It lives in whispered recommendations, borrowed equipment, old dubplates, battered flight cases, and the stories that pass between generations in the gaps between sets. This is the ineffable Stroud connection not as myth for branding, but as a real social weave of local memory, practical resourcefulness and collective appetite. It is there in the line between the smoking area and the booth. It is there in the old forgotten lockups of record collections and rave gear. It is there in the knowledge that scenes survive because somebody keeps the cables, remembers the route, lends the monitors, or passes on the right plate at the right time.
	    </div>
	</div>
	<div class="secondary-background">
	    <?php
	    renderPageBreak(1, 'primary');
	    ?>
	    <h3>Routes, not roots. Or rather: roots carried through routes.</h3>
	</div>
	<?php renderPageBreak(2, 'secondary'); ?>
	<br>
	<div class="paragraph">
	    <div>
	    Folk culture is not nostalgic. It is not heritage for display. It is living practice. It belongs to those who participate in it, who sustain it, who pass it on through doing. It shifts with each generation, absorbing new technologies, new sounds and new conditions while holding onto something essential: a way of gathering, a way of organising space, a way of being together.
	    <hr>
	    <span class="ref-content"><span class="ref">1. </span>For the sonic version of this argument, see Simon Reynolds, Energy Flash: A Journey Through Rave Music and Dance Culture; and for the social and political history of dancing in Britain, Ed Gillett, Party Lines: Dance Music and the Making of Modern Britain. Paul Sullivan’s Remixology: Tracing the Dub Diaspora remains essential in understanding dub not just as a style but as a method and an infrastructure of circulation.</span>
	    </div>
