	<br>
	<h2>II. The Stroud Node</h2>
	<br>
	<div class="paragraph">
	    <span>Stroud sits within its own lineage. The South West has long held a particular relationship to rave: fields, rural infrastructures, travelling systems and temporary autonomy. A different scale, a different pace, but part of the same wider network. Culture is always located.</span><span>Seasoning is built from this place. It is local and open at the same time, a meeting point between Stroud and the wider underground, between emerging artists and established ones, between different scenes, different communities and different ways of understanding sound. This localism is not defensive. It is what Stuart Hall would call positional: rooted enough to speak clearly, open enough to keep articulation alive.</span><span>Seasoning’s public history shows that it has already been building this weave for years. The <a href="events">event archive</a> runs from early SVA parties in 2022 through Studio 18, The Goods Shed, Bristol link-ups, solidarity fundraisers and on into the 2026 full weekend festival. That timeline matters because it shows continuity: the same Stroud nucleus gradually learning how to host bigger conversations without losing its own grain.</span><span>Lovellious appears right at the start in the <a href="event/20220402">first SVA event in April 2022</a> and keeps reappearing across the project’s history, which makes the <strong>Love Cuts</strong> axis especially important. Love Cuts is not just an alias or side project. It is a practice of edits, reworks and rethinks, flipping restless soul, eskibeat, jungle and broken beat into new club forms. That is folk process in miniature: not origin myths, but practical versioning, the passing on of materials through touch and use.</span>
	    <div></div><div class="figure-caption">A local edit is a folk form. So is a dubplate. So is a city-to-city link that still feels handmade.</div>
	    <span>What we are trying to build, then, is not a weekend away from life. It is a durable scene inside life. Something earthy, bass-led, porous and strange. Something that can still be felt in the smoking area after dawn, and in the knowledge that this music only stays alive if people keep gathering to pass it on.</span>
	</div>
	<div class="secondary-background">
