		<span style="flex-basis: 100%;" class="ref-content"><span class="ref">3 </span>Fred Moten is useful here because the stack can be read as a site of fugitive planning: an improvised sociality that official categories are never quite able to contain.</span><span>The manifesto also has to name the <strong>Stroud Cypher</strong>, because the cypher reveals the heart of the whole project. A cypher is not merely a showcase. It is a format of relation. People gather in a circle, test one another, encourage one another, inherit and improvise at once. That is exactly what a local festival should be doing if it takes seriously the claim that rave is folk culture</span><span>This is why Seasoning keeps circling sounds that are not always expected to meet under the same roof: UK techno, 140, bruk pressure, rap, diasporic dance music, downtempo drift and low-end psychedelia. Not as mood-board eclecticism, but as a practical social question. What happens when these people, frequencies, memories and energies are allowed to occupy the same temporary village?</span><span>The answer is not unity in the thin sense. It is the chance for overlap. A chance for the dancefloor to become a commons where different histories can be held in proximity without being dissolved into content. Here the project touches both <i>deep ecology</i> and <i>social ecology</i>. The first names a respect for place, weather, terrain, mud, darkness, industrial edge, valley acoustics and the more-than-human conditions within which a festival is actually held. The second names the mesh of relations that make the gathering possible at all: friends of friends, crews, venues, volunteers, sound techs, neighbours, local authorities, local sceptics, old heads, first-timers and all the whispered trust that turns a plan into a room.</span>
		</div>
	</div>
