	<br>
	<h2>VII. Theses for a Local Festival</h2>
	<br>
	<div class="paragraph">
	    <ol>
		<li>Rave culture is folk culture because it is carried in use, not in branding</li>
		<li>It survives through rigs, edits, rewinds, favours, conversations and repeated gatherings.</li>
		<li>A town like Stroud can hold dubstep, bruk, techno, rap, diasporic dance music and ambient drift in the same hand without reducing them to content.</li>
		<li>Local scenes become durable when they are allowed to be porous.</li>
		<li>Hospitality matters because the room has to remain open to strangers without demanding sameness in return.</li>
		<li>A soundsystem is a teacher.</li>
		<li>Parties like Club Blanco, Cabin Fever, Spirit, JoyLift, Love Cuts, How We Met, Immigrant Party and Brown Excellence show different ways of keeping the commons alive. Seasoning’s task is not to imitate them but to let their energies cross-pollinate inside its own South West ecology.</li>
		<li>Escarpment futurism names a Stroud-situated horizon: a future built from edges, ridgelines and passing routes rather than from capitals alone.</li>
	    </ol>
	    <span style="flex-basis: 100%;">This is the belief in full. Rave culture is folk culture because it is carried by participation, shaped by place and sustained through collective effort. It belongs to those who keep it moving.</span>
	</div>
